                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "[b]the grim ending[/b] is a ~1.5-hour 3rd-person point & click adventure. it looks and feels like the classics, with commands like [i]look at[/i], [i]take[/i], etc. none of the modern-day conveniences of simply clicking stuff to examine or pick up, or double-click to move faster, but right click cycles through available commands.\n\nit's [b]fully and badly voiced[/b] (removing all the annoying noises would improve things considerably) and lets you [b]save manually anytime, but initially only over a single slot[/b]. you know, unlike games from 30 fucking years ago with as many saves as you wanted. there are [b]multiple endings[/b] based on decisions, which made the single save slot even worse, but [b]5 additional slots got patched in after this review got posted[/b].\n\nthe story involves the main character waking up in hell. the boss is annoying as hell as well and the horrible voiceovers don't help. you're tasked with taking pictures of stuff, which in turn makes them disappear. I guess that's one way to clean up all the colors and living things on a gray landscape that is the land of the dead. eventually you start to wise up and can decide how to go about certain things. with multiple saves I would've saved regularly and tried different things, but as is, I went with what I thought best, plus reloaded the save before the end to check another conclusion. good enough.\n\nthe game was initially and insanely priced at $15, which got permanently halved shortly after. for that much money, or any money really, it's [b]not unreasonable to expect at least some settings, but there are none[/b]. can't set volumes or toggle the awful speech, music or subtitles, nothing. subtitles are always on, often with [b]missing punctuation and lots of grammar mistakes[/b]. apparently, we're lucky to have alt+enter put the game in a smaller, non-resizable window, but that doesn't have much to do with the developer.\n\notherwise it's [b]good enough for a quick point & click fix, but the price point is still too high[/b], especially considering how lacking it is functionality-wise. nothing wrong with trying to imitate olden times, but when [b]decades later you're offering less than those games, it's laughable[/b]. only on deep sale.",
